WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:02.380
Speaker 1: Let's do some good news today.

00:00:02.380 --> 00:00:05.510
We are really starting to crush the coronavirus,
my friends.

00:00:05.510 --> 00:00:06.510
We are really doing it.

00:00:06.510 --> 00:00:08.260
Check out some of these beautiful numbers.

00:00:08.260 --> 00:00:09.890
Now, this doesn't mean it's over.

00:00:09.890 --> 00:00:12.040
People, they're still suffering.

00:00:12.040 --> 00:00:13.600
People are still dying.

00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:17.490
But we are seeing numbers go very much in
the correct direction.

00:00:17.490 --> 00:00:23.090
Coronavirus cases in the United States, which
peaked in mid-January at two hundred fifty

00:00:23.090 --> 00:00:29.000
five thousand cases per day, are now down
to twenty five thousand cases per day.

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:35.989
That's a decline of more than 90 percent in
New Delhi cases.

00:00:35.989 --> 00:00:43.740
Coronavirus deaths, which peaked in late January
at more than thirty four hundred a day.

00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:47.540
9/11 plus 10 percent every day.

00:00:47.540 --> 00:00:50.470
That's where we were in late January.

00:00:50.470 --> 00:00:55.240
Those are now down below five hundred and
fifty a day, the lowest numbers going all

00:00:55.240 --> 00:00:57.810
the way back to July of twenty twenty.

00:00:57.810 --> 00:00:59.210
Still too many.

00:00:59.210 --> 00:01:06.399
But importantly, once we get down to about
300 a day, you're really talking about something

00:01:06.399 --> 00:01:10.520
that has gotten below the level of your typical
flu season.

00:01:10.520 --> 00:01:13.450
We're not there yet, but we are going in that
direction now.

00:01:13.450 --> 00:01:19.509
In the more heavily vaccinated states, the
numbers of covid deaths are already down to

00:01:19.509 --> 00:01:23.170
roughly the average flu deaths in a normal
flu season.

00:01:23.170 --> 00:01:28.290
As an example in the state or commonwealth
of Massachusetts, on many recent days, they

00:01:28.290 --> 00:01:35.899
are seeing seven covid deaths, five covid
deaths, zero one day to another 12 another.

00:01:35.899 --> 00:01:41.429
In a normal flu season, you see four to five
flu deaths a day in Massachusetts.

00:01:41.429 --> 00:01:42.880
We are getting down to that level.

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:47.560
In some states, this does not mean it's over,
but it does mean that at the current level

00:01:47.560 --> 00:01:51.250
of vaccination, we're getting very close to
crushing it completely.

00:01:51.250 --> 00:01:57.289
Now, as a reminder, while we are seeing this
level of case decline while only having thirty

00:01:57.289 --> 00:02:03.569
nine percent of the US population totally
vaccinated, we are going to see a much more

00:02:03.569 --> 00:02:08.020
significant decline as we get to 50 and beyond.

00:02:08.020 --> 00:02:11.930
Percent of vaccinated people cases have already
dropped 90 percent.

00:02:11.930 --> 00:02:15.599
We are only thirty nine percent fully vaccinated
in the United States.

00:02:15.599 --> 00:02:20.469
When we get to 50 percent vaccination, these
numbers will be even lower.

00:02:20.469 --> 00:02:22.629
I think we will have no trouble getting to
50.

00:02:22.629 --> 00:02:28.450
I think we'll have not much trouble getting
to 60 percent once we get beyond 60.

00:02:28.450 --> 00:02:33.430
It's going to depend on how much anti vaccine
sentiment we can do away with because that

00:02:33.430 --> 00:02:34.430
will be a problem.

00:02:34.430 --> 00:02:36.400
There is going to be this limit.

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:40.709
Roughly 20 percent of the country doesn't
want the vaccine.

00:02:40.709 --> 00:02:44.620
And you have to assume that on top of that,
there are people who sort of casually want

00:02:44.620 --> 00:02:48.939
it, but they won't go and actually get it.

00:02:48.939 --> 00:02:52.329
So that is going to be a challenge once we
get beyond 60 percent.

00:02:52.329 --> 00:02:56.400
But by the Fourth of July, things are going
to be looking really, really good.

00:02:56.400 --> 00:03:01.890
Now, as a reminder, my prediction was under
twenty thousand cases a day by Memorial Day.

00:03:01.890 --> 00:03:02.920
We're five days away.

00:03:02.920 --> 00:03:04.030
I think it's going to be close.

00:03:04.030 --> 00:03:05.530
I think we're going to be right there.

00:03:05.530 --> 00:03:10.120
We essentially we're at twenty five a day
right now and we have five days to go.

00:03:10.120 --> 00:03:16.980
So we need to shed a thousand cases a day
between now and Memorial Day.

00:03:16.980 --> 00:03:23.560
Lastly, of course, this is a global pandemic
and it's a global effort.

00:03:23.560 --> 00:03:30.379
I'm glad to finally be able to say the global
cases and deaths are continuing down from

00:03:30.379 --> 00:03:36.840
the global peak of about eight hundred and
thirty thousand cases per day in early April.

00:03:36.840 --> 00:03:42.370
We are now down to about five hundred and
eighty thousand cases per day globally.

00:03:42.370 --> 00:03:49.900
Still a crazy number, but a decline of about
30 percent in global cases.

00:03:49.900 --> 00:03:55.049
Global deaths down about 17 percent from their
peak, still lagging.

00:03:55.049 --> 00:03:58.260
No doubt a lot of people are dying.

00:03:58.260 --> 00:04:04.150
But you'd rather be at this number and heading
down than at this number and heading up.

00:04:04.150 --> 00:04:06.590
So let's keep things going.

00:04:06.590 --> 00:04:08.730
Let's keep getting ourselves vaccinated.

00:04:08.730 --> 00:04:11.420
We are really seeing the light at the end
of the tunnel.

00:04:11.420 --> 00:04:15.950
We had a great conversation yesterday with
infectious disease doctor Isaac BOE.

00:04:15.950 --> 00:04:18.269
Gosh, he was optimistic.

00:04:18.269 --> 00:04:21.549
Check that out if you missed yesterday's podcast.

00:04:21.549 --> 00:04:23.110
And we are going to have to now.

00:04:23.110 --> 00:04:29.460
I mean, listen, I know I say to say this,
we are the envy of the world in terms of vaccine

00:04:29.460 --> 00:04:30.919
availability.

00:04:30.919 --> 00:04:33.080
There are countries desperate for vaccines.

00:04:33.080 --> 00:04:36.920
They get one hundred thousand and they are
thrilled.

00:04:36.920 --> 00:04:38.889
And we have millions and millions.

00:04:38.889 --> 00:04:41.340
We've we've already done hundreds of millions
of doses.

00:04:41.340 --> 00:04:42.440
They're available.

00:04:42.440 --> 00:04:43.550
Just walk in.

00:04:43.550 --> 00:04:45.889
It's hard it's hard not to get a vaccine at
this point.

00:04:45.889 --> 00:04:50.819
You can get them so many places and still
so many people are just not doing it.

00:04:50.819 --> 00:04:51.819
Let's do that.

00:04:51.819 --> 00:04:53.560
We're going in the right direction.

00:04:53.560 --> 00:04:54.669
Let's do it.

00:04:54.669 --> 00:05:00.370
However, there are still deaths, despite what
